ORIGIN

This skill is original work. It was first distributed under the name
"interpretive-ambiguity-stress-test", as one half of the "ambiguity-audit" plugin, alongside
a companion skill (ambiguity-report) that renders a completed audit as a website, Word
document, slide deck, or LaTeX/PDF. This standalone release performs the analysis only; it
does not produce formatted deliverables.

Instructions that previously lived at the plugin level — the rule for choosing between the
judicial-opinion profile's two output modes, and the citation-checking stage — have been
folded into this skill so that it runs correctly on its own.

The plugin bundled two legal-research connectors. A skill cannot carry connectors, so this
release instead adapts to whatever research tools the user has: an explicitly named source,
any connected legal-research tool, ordinary web search, or the text alone, with the choice
disclosed in the output.

---

DISCLAIMER

Audits produced by this skill are interpretive analyses of a legal text. They are not legal
advice, they do not determine anyone's rights or obligations, and predicted outcomes are
predictions rather than answers. Users remain responsible for reading the instrument, for
verifying any authority named, and for confirming that a cited case or audited opinion
remains good law.
